**Verdict: ACCEPT.** The six fixes and the refusal do what review 62 asked; nothing in the diff breaks. Six minor items follow.

### 1. F62-1
- **Numbers:** none left. `.parts` is read only in `precedence` (`version.rs:70-73`); six sites ask `same_release` (`mod.rs:1048, 1076, 1212, 1232, 1255, 1290`), three ask `is_newer_than` (`mod.rs:906, 1364, 1366`).
- **Parser:** two releases read as one only through the leading `v` or the trailing `.`. One release can read as two (tag case, no hyphen, leading zeros, build metadata); the first three order equal, so an update says `up to date` and never compares identities.
- **Real engines:** no break by reading: codex, kimi and grok pin the checked text (`engine.rs:257-293`); agy has no check (`engine.rs:66-70`). Unproven: a CLI that prints its release otherwise than the vendor reads it (RC1).
- One behaviour change is not in the CHANGELOG: finding #2.

### 2. F62-3
Nothing found. One path: `provenance` (`web.rs:162-175`), called by `published_from` (`models.rs:1044`), called by `published_ids` (`models.rs:1099`), used at `classes.rs:246, 323`. The built-in exception needs no `url` and the exact built-in name; `url` without a status, or unlike the name, is withheld (`web.rs:172`). No fixture was bent: the converted ones show what the current build writes, and the read-while-private shape keeps its own negatives (`classes.rs:1303-1310`, `telemetry_parity.rs:1169`). Cost: finding #6.

### 3. Other fixes
- **F62-2:** does her remedy (`providers.rs:2569`), opens nothing. Her RC2 named consult, panel and detach; only `c3 work` gets an integration test, and that is not this commit's code: finding #3.
- **F62-4:** does it (`contain.rs:848-891`); without hard links it falls to a 32-hex name and a rename. Nothing new.
- **F62-5:** does it (`quota.rs:1112-1117`). Nothing new.
- **F62-6:** does it (`reviewed.rs:785-793`); briefs equal in their first 120 bytes share a stem, the digest tells them apart. Nothing new.
- **Refusal:** both callers turn it into a line, neither retries (`models.rs:1237-1239`, `repo_models.rs:543-545`). Old wording survives: finding #1.

### 4. Tests that pass without their fix
`work.rs:2431` (finding #3). By my reading of the removed lines, every other new test fails at `ea30cce`. Proved by no test: findings #4 and #6.

### Findings
- **#1 minor** - eight places still say the file is replaced by rename without an exchange: `README.md:623-624` and seven doc comments (`contain.rs:675`, `models.rs:1161`, `repo_models.rs:19, 65, 459`, `rules.rs:153, 328`).
- **#2 minor** - `update did not take` (`mod.rs:1048-1057`) no longer smokes the release the updater left; it waits for the next pass (`mod.rs:270-273, 1933`). At `ea30cce` a release with equal or newer numbers was smoked and rolled back at once.
- **#3 minor** - the new `work.rs` test runs `c3 work`, unchanged here (`work/mod.rs:204`); `providers.rs:2569` has only the unit test `state_dir/tests.rs:986-1003`.
- **#4 note** - no test drives `mod.rs:1048`, `mod.rs:1076` or `engine.rs:154` with a same-numbers release (`maintain.rs:484, 501` use older numbers).
- **#5 note** - the F57-2 doc comment now sits over the new test (`quota.rs:4478-4483`).
- **#6 note** - a built-in record without status goes through real telemetry in no binary test; only `models.rs:2242-2249` covers it.

### Not read
The other c3-cli test files; `run_dirs`. Nothing was run.

## Requested checks
- **RC1** (read-only, network, 1 min): with codex, kimi and grok current, compare each `--version` with its vendor reading (`npm view @openai/codex version`, the kimi `latest` marker, `grok update --check --json`). A pair differing beyond a leading `v` or trailing `.` settles question 1.
- **RC2** (Linux CI, temp repo, workspace-write, 5 min): symlink `l` out of the repo; `c3 consult --collab-dir l/../.collab4` with a fake reviewer. Record inside, outside `.collab4` empty: finding #3.
- **RC3** (workspace-write, 10 min): fake grok writes `1.0.80-rc.1` for `update --version 1.0.80`; smoke `pass-then-fail`. `did not take`, no rollback, record kept: finding #2.
- **RC4** (workspace-write, 15 min): on a file system without `RENAME_EXCHANGE`, a grok swap against the fake. Refusal line, roster unchanged, no temp left: covers `contain.rs:774`, which no test drives.
- **RC5** (one build): revert `mod.rs:1048` and `:1076` to a numeric comparison; `cargo test -p c3-cli --test maintain` still green: finding #4.

### Findings

- **F65-1** [minor] `README.md:623`, `crates/c3/src/contain.rs:675`, `crates/c3/src/maintain/models.rs:1161`, `crates/c3/src/setup/repo_models.rs:19`, `crates/c3/src/setup/repo_models.rs:65`, `crates/c3/src/setup/repo_models.rs:459`, `crates/c3/src/setup/rules.rs:153`, `crates/c3/src/setup/rules.rs:328` - Eight documentation places still say that where there is no atomic exchange the roster or the repaired file is replaced by rename and the line says so. This commit removed that fallback (the write is refused) and updated README.md:818-819, so README.md:623-624 now states the opposite of what c3 maintain does on such a file system. Trigger: An operator on a file system without RENAME_EXCHANGE or ReplaceFileW reads the README's roster paragraph, or a reader compares these doc comments with contain.rs:726-740 and 774. Evidence: read-code: The sentence 'on a system without an atomic exchange the roster is replaced by rename and the report says so' is still there.; read-code: publish_exchange has no rename fallback: the refuse closure returns an Unsupported error and removes the temp file; Exchange::Unsupported returns refuse(why).; read-code: The same stale clause in each: 'published by rename', 'replaced by rename (no atomic exchange here)'. Verify: From the repository root run grep -rn 'replaced by rename' README.md crates/c3/src and grep -rn 'published by rename' crates/c3/src (read-only): eight hits, each naming the removed behaviour. Remedy: Reword the eight places as README.md:818-819 already reads: without an atomic exchange the file is not written and the line says so.
- **F65-2** [minor] `crates/c3/src/maintain/mod.rs:1048`, `crates/c3/src/maintain/mod.rs:1054`, `crates/c3/src/maintain/mod.rs:270`, `crates/c3/src/maintain/mod.rs:1933` - When the updater leaves a readable release other than the pinned target (the same numbers with another identity, a build-metadata variant, or a newer one), update_and_verify now reports 'update did not take' and returns without smoking what is installed. The pending record is resolved only inside the next pass, which the schedule puts an interval away, so that release serves unsmoked meanwhile and is not rolled back if it is broken. At ea30cce a release whose numbers were equal to or newer than the target was smoked in the same pass and rolled back on failure. Trigger: The vendor's updater or a concurrent manual update leaves a release whose text differs from the checked target. Evidence: read-code: Removed: !matches!(&after, Some(v) if v >= latest_v). Added: !after.as_ref().is_some_and(|v| v.same_release(latest_v)). The branch below returns before the smoke.; read-code: The did-not-take return is at 1054-1056; the post-update smoke starts at 1063.; read-code: A pending record is handled only inside a pass; next_due is +interval days (one day only when an engine was in use); the hook spawns a pass only when is_due holds, and a pending record does not make it due. Verify: RC3. Remedy: When the re-read version is a readable release other than the target, run the recovery smoke on it in the same pass, credited to itself (the third-version path of handle_pending_smoke) with the rollback on failure, instead of returning with the record kept.
- **F65-3** [minor] `crates/c3-cli/tests/work.rs:2431`, `crates/c3/src/work/mod.rs:204`, `crates/c3/src/providers.rs:2569`, `crates/c3/src/state_dir/tests.rs:986` - a_collab_path_with_dot_dot_after_a_link_is_taken_by_text runs c3 work, whose collab resolution this commit does not change (it has applied state_dir::lexical itself since F61-3), so the test passes at ea30cce as well. The code the commit changed, resolve_collab_root_with, is used by consult, panel, detach and a panel member and is covered only by a unit test that calls resolve_collab_root and begin_run directly; no test goes through those entry points, which is what review 62's RC2 asked for. Trigger: Delete providers.rs:2569 and run the c3-cli work tests: still green. Evidence: read-code: The test calls e.work(...), which runs c3 work with --collab-dir l/../.collab4.; read-code: c3 work joins the path, applies collab_dir and lexical at :204 itself, and never calls resolve_collab_root. work/mod.rs is not among the patch's files.; read-code: The fix (lexical before the link check and canonicalize) and its only test, which asserts the resolved path and calls begin_run on it. Verify: RC2. Remedy: Add a c3 consult case (fake reviewer) with --collab-dir l/../.collab to a c3-cli test, and label the work.rs test as F61-3's or drop it.
- **F65-4** [note] `crates/c3/src/maintain/mod.rs:1048`, `crates/c3/src/maintain/mod.rs:1076`, `crates/c3/src/maintain/engine.rs:154`, `crates/c3-cli/tests/maintain.rs:484`, `crates/c3-cli/tests/maintain.rs:501` - No test drives the two identity comparisons outside the recovery with a release of the target's numbers: an updater that leaves 1.0.80-rc.1 for the target 1.0.80 (mod.rs:1048), a rollback that restores a same-numbers release (mod.rs:1076), or codex's release date keyed by the exact release (engine.rs:154). The existing did-not-take and rollback tests use older numbers, which the numeric comparison refuses as well. Trigger: Revert mod.rs:1048 and :1076 to a numeric comparison: every test stays green. Evidence: read-code: The new tests exercise handle_pending_smoke, the update decision and the type; none reaches update_and_verify with a same-numbers release.; read-code: The no-op update leaves 1.0.77 for 1.0.80; the no-op rollback leaves 1.0.80 for 1.0.77. Verify: RC5. Remedy: Two fake-grok cases (updater writes 1.0.80-rc.1 for target 1.0.80; rollback writes 1.0.77-rc.1) asserting 'update did not take (still 1.0.80-rc.1)' and 'rollback failed, still 1.0.77-rc.1', and a codex time fixture keyed by a pre-release.
- **F65-5** [note] `crates/c3-core/src/quota.rs:4478` - The new F62-5 test was inserted between the doc comment of a_tool_inventory_is_complete_breached_or_unproven and that test, so the F57-2 comment (complete / breached / unproven) now documents the wrong test. Trigger: Reading the test module. Evidence: read-code: The '(F57-2) A tool inventory is COMPLETE only when ...' comment is followed by #[test] fn a_namespace_nested_in_the_multi_agent_namespace_keeps_its_ancestry. Verify: Read quota.rs:4478-4483 and the fn a_tool_inventory_is_complete_breached_or_unproven that follows the new test. Remedy: Move the new test below the old one or give it its own doc comment.
- **F65-6** [note] `crates/c3-cli/tests/telemetry_parity.rs:899`, `crates/c3/src/telemetry/classes.rs:1206`, `crates/c3/src/maintain/models.rs:2242` - The fixture records that used to be a built-in catalog record without a status (an earlier build's) now carry url and public: true. The one exception in provenance, a built-in record with no url trusted by its exact name (web.rs:174), is therefore exercised only by one unit assertion; no binary-level test sends such a record through telemetry any more. Trigger: A maintain state holding a URL-named built-in record without status (written by a build before F62-3) whose first fetch with the new build fails: whether its ids still publish. Evidence: read-code: Both built-in fixtures gained url and public: true.; read-code: builtin_legacy is the only remaining record without status; asserted at unit level. Verify: Add a record {source: web:https://openrouter.ai:443/api/v1/models, ids: [qwen/qwen4-max]} without url to the state of only_a_trusted_vendor_bound_published_id_is_sent_by_name and expect qwen4-max by name. Remedy: Keep one status-less built-in record in a binary-level telemetry test beside the status-carrying one.

